('9ad08526-7de7-50dd-9886-fda7a47a9f66'::uuid,'est','"Algebra Foundations, Equations & Inequalities"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to the system of inequalities given below?\nx + y < 4\nx² + y < 8"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"(1, 5)"},{"key":"G","text":"(1, 2)"},{"key":"H","text":"(4, −1)"},{"key":"J","text":"(−4, −1)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh.md","source_sha256":"f99122153ba873fb3a01697b0501e31119af3babff6a9ed2bc0baf050aed1f20","source_question_id":"MA-44","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Algebra Foundations, Equations & Inequalities"}'::jsonb,'"G"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh.md (MA-44)."'::jsonb),
('d2200fc0-a90f-530b-b5f8-af9242f7412b'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A rectangular parking lot measures 150 feet by 230 feet. The owner of the parking lot will expand the lot''s size by adding x feet and 2x feet to its dimensions, as shown below.\n[Figure: 150 ft by 230 ft rectangle; x added to the 150-ft side, 2x added to the 230-ft side.]\nIf the total area of the parking lot cannot exceed 40,000 square feet, which inequality can be used to find all possible values of x?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"(230 + 2x)(150 + x) ≤ 40,000"},{"key":"B","text":"(230 + x)(150 + 2x) ≤ 40,000"},{"key":"C","text":"(230 + 2x)(150 + x) ≥ 40,000"},{"key":"D","text":"(230 + x)(150 + 2x) ≥ 40,000"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh.md","source_sha256":"f99122153ba873fb3a01697b0501e31119af3babff6a9ed2bc0baf050aed1f20","source_question_id":"MA-45","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MA-45.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MA-45","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh.md (MA-45)."'::jsonb),
('065c9af9-a689-5887-8031-6ed01300d5f7'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Which type of transformation can be used to obtain the graph of g(x) = 4(2^x) from the graph of f(x) = 2^x ?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"Vertical shrink"},{"key":"G","text":"Vertical shift down"},{"key":"H","text":"Vertical shift up"},{"key":"J","text":"Vertical stretch"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh.md","source_sha256":"f99122153ba873fb3a01697b0501e31119af3babff6a9ed2bc0baf050aed1f20","source_question_id":"MA-46","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh.md (MA-46)."'::jsonb),
('f916071f-ed99-564e-98d1-4dd987bbce00'::uuid,'est','"Functions, Transformations & Graphs"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"What is the greatest value in the domain of the function graphed below?\n[Figure: Piecewise graph — segment from closed point (−5, 7) down to open point (0, −3), and segment from closed point (0, 4) to closed point (6, 2).]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"2"},{"key":"B","text":"6"},{"key":"C","text":"4"},{"key":"D","text":"7"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh.md","source_sha256":"f99122153ba873fb3a01697b0501e31119af3babff6a9ed2bc0baf050aed1f20","source_question_id":"MA-47","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Functions, Transformations & Graphs","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MA-47.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MA-47","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh.md (MA-47)."'::jsonb),
('e58ad9d5-e7e9-5046-a48c-0aad61a72306'::uuid,'est','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A function is graphed below.\n[Figure: Downward parabola with vertex (−1, 4), crossing the x-axis at −3 and 1.]\nWhich function is best represented by this graph?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"y = −(x + 1)² + 4"},{"key":"G","text":"y = −(x − 1)² + 4"},{"key":"H","text":"y = −x² + 4x + 3"},{"key":"J","text":"y = −x² − 4x + 3"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh.md","source_sha256":"f99122153ba873fb3a01697b0501e31119af3babff6a9ed2bc0baf050aed1f20","source_question_id":"MA-48","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MA-48.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MA-48","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"F"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh.md (MA-48)."'::jsonb),
('279e6fea-9933-5f28-a45c-d06f44a24fbb'::uuid,'est','"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The average speed of sound in air at t degrees Celsius can be found using the function below. The speed, s, is measured in meters per second.\ns = 20.05√(t + 273.15)\nIf sound is traveling in air at an average speed of 356.5 meters per second, which temperature is closest to the value of t?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"76°C"},{"key":"B","text":"503°C"},{"key":"C","text":"43°C"},{"key":"D","text":"309°C"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh.md","source_sha256":"f99122153ba873fb3a01697b0501e31119af3babff6a9ed2bc0baf050aed1f20","source_question_id":"MA-49","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Rational, Radical, Exponential & Logarithmic Functions"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh.md (MA-49)."'::jsonb),
('1d9f5215-a004-5061-a42d-0e56ecad89de'::uuid,'est','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"[Figure: Scatterplot \"Defective Parts\" — Week (x) vs Number of Defective Parts per 10,000 (y); points approximately (1, 255), (2, 213), (4, 155), (7, 95), (9, 72), (10, 62), decreasing curve trend.]\nA factory began producing new parts. Data were collected on the number of defective parts per 10,000 parts produced. The graph shown displays some of the data for the first 10 weeks of production. Based on the graph, during which week were approximately 130 defective parts per 10,000 produced?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"Week 3"},{"key":"G","text":"Week 5"},{"key":"H","text":"Week 6"},{"key":"J","text":"Week 8"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh.md","source_sha256":"f99122153ba873fb3a01697b0501e31119af3babff6a9ed2bc0baf050aed1f20","source_question_id":"MA-50","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MA-50.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MA-50","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"G"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh.md (MA-50)."'::jsonb),
('4f80670d-3e98-502e-9c4f-d9a9cefcea2f'::uuid,'est','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Segment CD has an endpoint at (2, −1) and a midpoint at (8, 3). Which measure is closest to the length of CD?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"20.4 units"},{"key":"B","text":"8.9 units"},{"key":"C","text":"14.4 units"},{"key":"D","text":"11.7 units"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-01","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-01)."'::jsonb),
('120df286-53e8-5183-a50c-62c7fed7c5d3'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Isosceles trapezoid JKLM is shown below.\n[Figure: Isosceles trapezoid JKLM with JK = 39 units (top base), ML = 56 units (bottom base), legs KL = JM = 17 units, angle J = 120°.]\nIf the dimensions of trapezoid JKLM are multiplied by a scale factor of f to create trapezoid J′K′L′M′, which statement is true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"Trapezoid J′K′L′M′ contains two base angles measuring 30° each."},{"key":"G","text":"The longer base of trapezoid J′K′L′M′ is 56f units."},{"key":"H","text":"The bases of trapezoid J′K′L′M′ have lengths of 22 units and 39 units."},{"key":"J","text":"Trapezoid J′K′L′M′ contains two base angles measuring (120f)° each."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-02","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-02.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-02","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"G"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-02)."'::jsonb),
('58d620c6-e2b4-5636-a529-f0515a6eb138'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In the diagram below, the angle of depression from P to Q is 45°.\n[Figure: Cliff with point P at the top; Q on the ground 32 ft from the base of the cliff; dashed horizontal line from P and dashed line of sight PQ. Not drawn to scale.]\nWhich of the following is closest to the distance between P and Q?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"45.3 ft"},{"key":"G","text":"22.6 ft"},{"key":"H","text":"55.4 ft"},{"key":"J","text":"18.5 ft"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-04","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-04.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-04","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"F"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-04)."'::jsonb),
('237f5ac0-7798-5b80-856f-4b522ff4404f'::uuid,'est','"Solid Geometry & Volume"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The rectangular pyramid shown below was intersected by a plane parallel to base ABCD to form quadrilateral A′B′C′D′.\n[Figure: Pyramid with apex F and rectangular base ABCD; plane parallel to the base cuts the lateral edges at A′, B′, C′, D′.]\nBased on this information, which statement cannot be proved true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"ABCD ~ A′B′C′D′"},{"key":"B","text":"AF/A′F = BF/B′F"},{"key":"C","text":"∠AA′D′ ≅ ∠BB′A′"},{"key":"D","text":"∠BCD ≅ ∠B′C′D′"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-05","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Solid Geometry & Volume","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-05.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-05","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-05)."'::jsonb),
('cd1d8d99-db52-54f4-ac37-c8d2dfdcb230'::uuid,'est','"Solid Geometry & Volume"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Five spheres are being painted for a display at a store. If the diameter of each sphere is 7 centimeters, which value is closest to the total surface area that will be painted?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"770 cm²"},{"key":"G","text":"154 cm²"},{"key":"H","text":"192 cm²"},{"key":"J","text":"440 cm²"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-06","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Solid Geometry & Volume"}'::jsonb,'"F"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-06)."'::jsonb),
('cc7dbc9d-694b-5eab-9ae0-cc7ebd671131'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"On the map below, Main Street, 10th Street, and Highway 1 intersect to form a right triangle.\n[Figure: Right triangle with Highway 1 vertical, 10th Street horizontal (right angle between them), Main Street as hypotenuse making a 40° angle with 10th Street.]\nThe distance between 10th Street and Main Street along Highway 1 is 5.6 mi. Which measure is closest to the length of Main Street from Highway 1 to 10th Street?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"8.7 mi"},{"key":"B","text":"3.6 mi"},{"key":"C","text":"4.7 mi"},{"key":"D","text":"7.3 mi"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-07","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-07.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-07","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-07)."'::jsonb),
('3ade556c-1859-54c8-801b-9cf78dbccf91'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"For triangles ABC and DEF, ∠A ≅ ∠D and ∠B ≅ ∠E. Based on this information, which statement is a reasonable conclusion?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"∠C ≅ ∠D because they are corresponding angles of congruent triangles."},{"key":"G","text":"CA ≅ FD because they are corresponding parts of congruent triangles."},{"key":"H","text":"∠C ≅ ∠F because they are corresponding angles of similar triangles."},{"key":"J","text":"AB ≅ DE because they are corresponding parts of similar triangles."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-08","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"}'::jsonb,'"H"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-08)."'::jsonb),
('bb24f5ae-38dd-5175-8cc9-e7ccd48fe3ac'::uuid,'est','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Ray ZX and ray WY are secants of circle V, as shown below.\n[Figure: Circle with center V; secants ZX and WY intersect inside the circle at point U; arc WZ = 72°, arc XY = 60°.]\nBased on this information, which of the following can be proved true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"m∠ZUY = m∠ZVY"},{"key":"B","text":"m∠XUY = ½(mWZ + mXY)"},{"key":"C","text":"mWX = 180° − mWZ"},{"key":"D","text":"m∠WUX = ½(mZY − mWX)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-09","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-09.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-09","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-09)."'::jsonb),
('ee64c01a-0f12-5994-8586-9922849c65e2'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','grid_in','"Segment PQ is shown on the coordinate grid below. The coordinates of P and Q are integers.\n[Figure: Horizontal segment from P(−4, 2) to Q(−1, 2).]\nPoint (x, y) lies on the perpendicular bisector of PQ. What is the value of x?\nRecord your answer and fill in the bubbles on your answer document. [Griddable — answer: −2.5]"'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-10","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-10.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-10","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"−2.5"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-10)."'::jsonb),
('c923de85-c787-50b1-96b5-6106f8157742'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A conditional statement is given below.\n\"If two interior angles of a triangle are acute, then the third interior angle must be obtuse.\"\nWhich of the following best describes this statement?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"This statement is true because all obtuse triangles have two acute interior angles."},{"key":"B","text":"This statement is false because the third interior angle must also be acute."},{"key":"C","text":"This statement is true because a triangle can have at most one interior obtuse angle."},{"key":"D","text":"This statement is false because the third interior angle can be acute, right, or obtuse."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-11","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-11)."'::jsonb),
('a90e33b2-1dcb-59a7-b2c6-46d4f75d4f22'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The side length of a smaller square is one-third the side length of a larger square. Which of the following statements describes the area of the smaller square?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"The area of the smaller square is 1/27 the area of the larger square."},{"key":"G","text":"The area of the smaller square is 1/6 the area of the larger square."},{"key":"H","text":"The area of the smaller square is 1/9 the area of the larger square."},{"key":"J","text":"The area of the smaller square is 1/3 the area of the larger square."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-12","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"}'::jsonb,'"H"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-12)."'::jsonb),
('b3f6019d-f15d-52ec-b15b-291bcb48b989'::uuid,'est','"Sequences, Complex Numbers & Advanced Algebra"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A pattern of dots is shown in the four figures below.\n[Figure: Figure 1 has 2 dots; Figure 2 has 5 dots; Figure 3 has 8 dots; Figure 4 has 11 dots.]\nIf the pattern continues, which expression can be used to find the total number of dots in Figure n?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"3n − 5"},{"key":"B","text":"2n + 2"},{"key":"C","text":"2n + 3"},{"key":"D","text":"3n − 1"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-13","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Sequences, Complex Numbers & Advanced Algebra","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-13.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-13","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-13)."'::jsonb),
('9c70734c-242a-5a43-9db0-fe8b2e83d489'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The equation of a line containing one leg of a right triangle is y = −4x. Which of the following equations could represent the line containing the other leg of this triangle?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"y = −(1/4)x"},{"key":"G","text":"y = (1/4)x + 2"},{"key":"H","text":"y = 4x"},{"key":"J","text":"y = −4x + 2"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-14","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"}'::jsonb,'"G"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-14)."'::jsonb),
('6e928acf-674a-5eaa-9c4c-14f18288db78'::uuid,'est','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Two regular hexagons with center C and apothems a and b are shown in the figure below. Each vertex of the smaller hexagon is a midpoint on a side of the larger hexagon.\n[Figure: Two concentric regular hexagons; the region between them is shaded; apothem a drawn to a side of the larger hexagon, apothem b drawn to a side of the smaller hexagon.]\nIf a = 12√3 cm and b = 18 cm, what is the total area of the shaded regions?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"648√3 cm²"},{"key":"B","text":"36√3 cm²"},{"key":"C","text":"216√3 cm²"},{"key":"D","text":"1,512√3 cm²"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-15","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-15.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-15","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-15)."'::jsonb),
('e89a3f47-f39a-5c6f-bc2e-d2022316c8db'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'hard','mcq','"The two conditional statements below are true.\n\"If ∠3 and ∠4 form a linear pair, then they are supplementary. If ∠3 and ∠4 are supplementary, then m∠3 + m∠4 = 180°.\"\nBased on these conditional statements, which statement must also be true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"If ∠3 and ∠4 form a linear pair, then m∠3 + m∠4 = 180°."},{"key":"G","text":"If ∠3 and ∠4 form a linear pair, then m∠3 = 90°, and m∠4 = 90°."},{"key":"H","text":"If m∠3 + m∠4 = 180°, then ∠3 and ∠4 form a linear pair."},{"key":"J","text":"If ∠3 and ∠4 are supplementary, then ∠3 and ∠4 form a linear pair."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-16","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"}'::jsonb,'"F"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-16)."'::jsonb),
('05ac2162-b0b1-5079-9e84-5b3051e59140'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Rectangle STUW is shown below.\n[Figure: Rectangle STUW with S top-left, T top-right, U bottom-right, W bottom-left; point V on WU with VU = 5 in.; SV = 20 in.; VT = 8 in.]\nWhat is ST?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"33 in."},{"key":"B","text":"24 in."},{"key":"C","text":"√464 in."},{"key":"D","text":"√361 in."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-17","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-17.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-17","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-17)."'::jsonb),
('6feb3f66-234b-5029-8839-fd8c1f4f95c5'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"A statement is given below.\n\"The number of square units in the area of a square is greater than or equal to the number of units in the perimeter of the square.\"\nWhich side length of a square provides a counterexample to the given statement?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"6 units"},{"key":"G","text":"4 units"},{"key":"H","text":"10 units"},{"key":"J","text":"2 units"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-18","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-18)."'::jsonb),
('d5bd2849-fc8b-56bb-8bf0-dfd571ea433c'::uuid,'est','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The table below contains a pattern formed by the number of sides and the measure in degrees of each exterior angle of several regular convex polygons.\n[Table — Exterior Angle Measures of Regular Polygons. Number of Sides: 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 15, 20, 45, 90. Exterior Angle Measure (degrees): 120, 90, 72, 60, 36, 24, 18, 8, 4.]\nIf the pattern in the table continues, which statement is true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"As the number of sides in the polygon increases by 1, the measure of each exterior angle decreases by 25%."},{"key":"B","text":"The product of the number of sides in the polygon and the measure of each exterior angle is a constant."},{"key":"C","text":"The measure of each exterior angle is a multiple of 6 degrees."},{"key":"D","text":"If the number of sides in the polygon is even, the measure of each exterior angle is a multiple of 3 degrees."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-19","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-19)."'::jsonb),
